<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  vitals LaunchAgent template.

  Placeholders, substituted at install time by scripts/service-install.sh into
  ~/Library/LaunchAgents/local.vitals.daemon.plist:
    __NODE_BIN__  absolute path to node (a launchd job gets a bare PATH)
    __NODE_DIR__  the directory holding it, prepended to PATH
    __REPO__      absolute path to this repo
    __TSX__       absolute path to the tsx ESM loader in __REPO__/node_modules
    __LOG_DIR__   absolute log directory

  A user LaunchAgent, not a root LaunchDaemon. Nothing here needs privilege:
  4780 and 4781 are both unprivileged, and a process whose entire job is
  accepting a phone's health exports has no business owning root.

  node is exec'd directly rather than through `npm start`, so the job owns
  exactly one process and a termination signal reaches the server itself. It
  has a shutdown path -- close both listeners, checkpoint the WAL, close the
  store -- and routing the signal through a package-manager wrapper would
  leave that journal to luck.

  The loader is an ABSOLUTE path, not the bare `tsx` specifier that `npm
  start` can afford. A bare specifier resolves against the working directory,
  which makes the job one WorkingDirectory mistake away from a crash-loop that
  reads like a broken dependency.

  No --env-file flag, deliberately. `npm start` reads the repo's .env; this
  job does not, and any override for the service belongs in
  EnvironmentVariables below. Two reasons. A service whose configuration comes
  from a dotfile inside a git working copy is configuration nobody can see
  from the thing that starts it. And --env-file-if-exists is a node >= 22.9
  flag: on an older node that may also be installed, it degrades into a hard
  --env-file and the job dies with ".env: not found" while the same command
  works by hand. Carrying the flag also cost a permanent line on stderr on
  every start, which is exactly the noise that makes "the error log is empty"
  stop meaning anything.

  node 22 is still required -- better-sqlite3 is compiled against its ABI --
  and the installer proves that by loading the module with the binary it is
  about to write in here, rather than by reading a version string.

  The store path is deliberately NOT set here. src/config.ts derives it from
  $HOME (~/Library/Application Support/vitals/vitals.db) -- absolute, and the
  same whatever the working directory is. One daemon, one owner, one store:
  an override in this file would be a second path to keep in sync, and the
  failure it buys is two half-series nobody can compare.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>local.vitals.daemon</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>__NODE_BIN__</string>
        <string>--import</string>
        <string>__TSX__</string>
        <string>__REPO__/src/server.ts</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>__REPO__</string>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>__NODE_DIR__:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
        <key>NODE_ENV</key>
        <string>production</string>
    </dict>

    <key>LimitLoadToSessionType</key>
    <array>
        <string>Aqua</string>
        <string>Background</string>
    </array>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The property this job exists for: a crash comes back by itself. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- A missing credential or a held port exits non-zero on purpose. 30s
         keeps that readable in the log instead of turning it into a spin. -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>30</integer>

    <!-- Logs live outside the repo, next to the other services on this box.
         The store is already outside it; the daemon's runtime files have no
         reason to sit in a directory that gets committed and pushed. -->
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>__LOG_DIR__/vitals.log</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>__LOG_DIR__/vitals.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
